It is a shorter way of writing a lambda expression.

1️⃣ Normal Lambda Version
users.stream()
     .map(user -> user.getName())
     .toList();

Here:

user is an object of User

getName() method is called

name is returned

2️⃣ Method Reference Version
users.stream()
     .map(User::getName)
     .toList();

This means:

For each User object → call getName()

Java automatically understands that the stream contains User objects.

So internally it behaves like:

user -> user.getName()
3️⃣ Visual Flow
Stream<User>
      |
      | map(User::getName)
      v
Stream<String>

Example:

User Object	getName()
User("Ali")	"Ali"
User("Ahmed")	"Ahmed"

Result:

["Ali", "Ahmed"]
4️⃣ Why We Use Method Reference

Benefits:

✅ Cleaner code
✅ Less boilerplate
✅ More readable
✅ Functional style programming

5️⃣ Types of Method References (Important for Interviews)
1️⃣ Static Method
ClassName::staticMethod

Example:

Math::abs
2️⃣ Instance Method of Particular Object
object::method

Example:

System.out::println
3️⃣ Instance Method of Arbitrary Object
ClassName::method

Example:

User::getName
4️⃣ Constructor Reference
ClassName::new

Example:

ArrayList::new
6️⃣ Small Working Example
class User {
    private String name;

    public String getName() {
        return name;
    }
}

List<User> users = List.of(
        new User("Ali"),
        new User("Sara")
);

List<String> names = users.stream()
        .map(User::getName)
        .toList();

Output:

[Ali, Sara]
🔑 Interview One-Line Answer

:: is a method reference operator used to refer to a method without executing it, providing a shorter alternative to lambda expressions.

Example:

user -> user.getName()

becomes

User::getName

If you want, I can also explain a very tricky interview question:

Why does User::getName work in map() but getName() alone does not?


>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>

This question is very commonly asked in senior Java interviews.

Why does `User::getName` work in `map()` but `getName()` alone does not?

Because map() expects a Function, not the result of a function call.

1️⃣ What map() Actually Expects

The map() method in Stream API expects a Function.

<R> Stream<R> map(Function<? super T, ? extends R> mapper)

Meaning:

Input  -> Output
User   -> String

So map() needs something like:

Function<User, String>
2️⃣ Lambda Version (Correct)
users.stream()
     .map(user -> user.getName())

Here we pass a function:

User → String

Meaning:

take a user → return name
3️⃣ Method Reference Version (Also Correct)
users.stream()
     .map(User::getName)

User::getName means:

For any User object → call getName()

Java converts it internally to:

user -> user.getName()

So it still provides a Function<User, String>.

4️⃣ Why getName() Alone Fails

If you write:

users.stream()
     .map(getName())   // ❌ compile error

This means:

call getName() immediately

But:

getName() needs a User object

No object is provided

map() expects a function, not a value

So the compiler fails.